{"name":"Green Moray","hex":"#3a7968","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"moray","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the large cosmopolitan carnivorous eels of the family Muraenidae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moray"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green moray","blocks":[{"text":"The green moray is a moray eel of the family Muraenidae, found in the western Atlantic Ocean from Long Island, New York, to Bermuda, to the northern Gulf of Mexico and to Brazil, at depths down to 40 m (130 ft). With a length up to 2.5 m (8.2 ft), it is the largest moray species of the tropical Atlantic and one of the largest species of moray eels known."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/GreenMorayEel.JPG/330px-GreenMorayEel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_moray","wikidata":"Q1551970"},{"title":"Moray","blocks":[{"text":"Moray is one of the 32 council areas of Scotland. It lies in the north-east of the country, with a coastline on the Moray Firth, and borders the council areas of Aberdeenshire and Highland. Its council is based in Elgin, the area's largest town. The main towns are generally in the north of the area on the coastal plain. The south of the area is more sparsely populated and mountainous, including part of the Cairngorms National Park."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Moray_UK_location_map.svg/330px-Moray_UK_location_map.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moray","wikidata":"Q211106"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg/500px-Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg/1280px-Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg/500px-Green_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A green moray on the sand bed at 110' near the artificial reef Eagle in the Florida Keys National Marine Sanctuary","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Moray%2C_Florida_Keys.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Employmentmoray.png/500px-Employmentmoray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Employmentmoray.png/500px-Employmentmoray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"People employed in Moray (2003)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEmploymentmoray.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/GVAmoray.png/500px-GVAmoray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/GVAmoray.png/500px-GVAmoray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGVAmoray.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/IndustryGVAmray.png/500px-IndustryGVAmray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/IndustryGVAmray.png/500px-IndustryGVAmray.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndustryGVAmray.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/ManufacturingGVAmoray2.png/500px-ManufacturingGVAmoray2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/ManufacturingGVAmoray2.png/500px-ManufacturingGVAmoray2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AManufacturingGVAmoray2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/